     4	Let $\vars$ be a set of variables. We consider propositional formulas over variables in~$\vars$, in conjunctive normal form (CNF), that is, each formula has the form $\phi=\land_{i=1}^mc_i$. Moreover we see each clause as a set of literals.
     5	Furthermore, we consider 3-CNF formulas whose clauses contain at most three literals.
     6	The \emph{SAT problem} consists in deciding if a CNF formula is satisfiable, and the \emph{3-SAT} problem is the restriction of this problem to 3-CNF formulas.
     7	
     8	In this section, we present some NP-complete classes of 3-SAT problems from the literature, namely, the \emph{linear 3-SAT}, \emph{planar 3-SAT}, and \emph{monotone planar 3-SAT}.
     9	We will then define our own variants which combine features from these classes.
    10	
    11	\subsection{Linear 3-SAT}
    12	\label{subsection:linear}
    13	\import{figures_tikz/}{linear_sat_example.tex}
    14	
    15	A CNF formula $\phi$ is \emph{linear} if 
    16	1) each clause intersects with at most one other clause, and 
    17	2) each such intersection contains at most one literal.
    18	
    19	\begin{example}
    20		The formula $(a \lor b \lor \lnot c) \land (\lnot c \lor d \lor \lnot a) \land (\lnot b \lor c) \land (c \lor \lnot d)$ is linear. %
    21		 For instance $(a \lor b \lor \lnot c)$ intersects only with $(\lnot c \lor d \lor \lnot a)$ and the intersection is~$\{\lnot c\}$.
    22	\end{example}
    23	
    24	Linear \tsat is the restriction of 3-SAT to inputs that are linear 3-CNF. It is \npc  \cite{arkin2018selecting}.
    25	
    26	\subsection{Literal-Clause Graph and Variable-Clause Graph}
    27	
    28	\begin{definition}[Literal-Clause Graph] %
    29	    Let $\phi=\wedge_{i=1}^m c_i$ be a  CNF formula. The \emph{\litClausGraph of $\phi$}, denoted $\litClauGraphSign{\phi}$ is the bipartite graph where:
    30	    \begin{itemize}
    31	    	\item the vertices are the literals occurring in $\phi$ and the clauses in $\phi$;
    32	    	\item there is an edge between a literal $\ell$ and a clause $c_i$ iff $\ell$ appears in $c_i$.
    33	    \end{itemize}
    34	    \label{def:lit_claus_graph}
    35	\end{definition}
    36	
    37	
    38	
    39	
    40	
    41	
    42	\begin{definition}[Variable-Clause Graph]
    43	    Let $\phi=\wedge_{i=1}^m c_i$ a  CNF formula. The \emph{variable-clause graph of $\phi$}, denoted $\inciGraphSign{\phi}$, is the graph obtained from the \litClausGraph of $\phi$ by merging each literal vertex $\ell$ with $\neg \ell$.
    44	    \label{def:inci_graph}
    45	\end{definition}
    46	
    47	\begin{example}
    48		Figure~\ref{fig:literalclausegraph} shows both the literal-clause and variable-clause graphs of formula $(a \lor b \lor \lnot c) \land (\lnot c \lor d \lor \lnot a) \land (\lnot b \lor c) \land (c \lor \lnot d)$. A merged vertex is simply represented by the corresponding variable.
    49	\end{example}
    50	
    51	
    52	While variable-clause graphs appear in the literature (e.g \cite{lichtenstein1982planar})
    53	as \emph{incidence graphs}, we introduce literal-clause graphs here as a more refined version of variable-clause graphs.
    54	%
    55	We will use the term variable-clause graphs instead of incidence graphs to emphasize the relation to literal-clause graphs.
    56	
