    72	Let us now introduce a variant of linear planar \tsat formulas.
    73	Given a formula $\phi$ with variables $x_1, \dots, x_n$, a \emph{literal cycle} $\pi$ is a permutation of the literals $x_1, \lnot x_1, \dots, x_n, \lnot x_n$.
    74	Given a formula $\phi$ and a literal cycle~$\pi$, 
    75	the \emph{\litClausGraph of $\litClauGraphSign{\phi}$ augmented with $\pi$},
    76	denoted by $\litClauGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$, is obtained from 
    77	$\litClauGraphSign{\phi}$
    78	by adding the literal cycle~$\pi$, edges between opposite literals $\ell$ and $\neg \ell$ called \emph{paired edges}.
    79	
    80	The graph $\litClauGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$ is \emph{valid} if 
    81	there exists a planar embedding of $\litClauGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$,
    82	all paired edges are inside the cycle $\pi$, and all clauses are outside $\pi$.
    83	A 3-CNF formula is said to be \emph{literal-planar} if there exists a literal cycle $\pi$ 
    84	such that $\litClauGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$ is valid.
    85	
    86	%
