   105	        %
   106	        \hline 3-CNF Fragments & Features \\
   107	        \hline\hline \small Linear (Sect.~\ref{subsection:linear}) & Each clause $c$ intersects at most one other clause $c'$, such that $|c\cap c'| \leq 1$.\\
   108	        \hline \small Planar (Sect.~\ref{subsection:planar}) & $\exists$ variable cycle $\pi$ such that $\inciGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$ is planar.\\
   109	        \hline \small Monotone Planar (Sect.~\ref{subsection:mplanar}) & Planar, and each clause has only positive literals or only negative literals. The clauses that contains positive literals are separated from the clauses that contains negative literals by $\pi$.\\
   110	        \hline \bf \small Linear Planar (Sect.~\ref{subsection:lplanar}) & Linear and planar.\\
   111	        \hline \bf \small Linear Literal-Planar (Sect.~\ref{subsection:llplanar}) &
   112	        $\exists$ literal cycle $\pi$ such that $\litClauGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$ is planar and valid.
   113	        \\
   114	        \hline \bf \small Monotone Linear Planar (Def.~\ref{def:cmpl})& Linear, and monotone planar. \\
   115	        \hline
   116	    \end{tabular}
   117	    \end{center}
   118	    \label{table:tsat-summary}
   119	\end{table}
     1	\subsection{NP-completeness of \PLLPTSAT}
     2	\label{section:npc}
     3	
     4	In this section, we prove Theorem.~\ref{th:paired_planar_linear_tsat_npc}.
     5	To prove the NP-hardness of \PLLPTSAT, we construct a polynomial-time reduction from the \MPTSAT proved to be NP-complete \cite{de2010optimal}.
     6	
     7	\begin{theorem}
     8		\PLLPTSAT is \npc.
     9		\label{th:paired_planar_linear_tsat_npc}
    10	\end{theorem}
    11	
    12	%
    13	    Let $\phi$ be a monotone planar 3-CNF with $n$ positive clauses and $m$ negative clauses, and a corresponding variable cycle $\pi$. 
    14	    We consider an embedding of $\inciGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ where positive clauses are inside and negative clauses are outside the variable cycle.
    15	    
    16	    We denote by $V$ the set of variables and by $C$ the set of clauses occurring in $\phi$. 
    17	    For each variable $v$ in $V$,
    18	    we introduce fresh variables $p^v_i$ and $a^v_i$ for $1 \leq i \leq n$, and variables $n^v_j$ and $b^v_j$ for $1 \leq j \leq m$.
    19	
    20	
    21	    Intuitively, clauses of $\phi$ can have arbitrary intersections since $\phi$ is not assumed to be linear. Because we want to reduce the problem to 
    22	    the satisfaction of a planar literal-linear formula, we will replace all occurrences of the variables by fresh variables we just introduced,
    23	    in such a way that the resulting formula is literal-linear.
    24	
    25	    \newcommand{\phiprimer}{\psi'}
    26	    Formally, we define the 3-CNF formula  $\phiprimer$, of the same size as $\phi$, obtained as follows from $\phi$.
    27	    For all variables $v \in V$, 
    28	    we replace the $i$-th positive occurrence of $v$ by $\lnot p^{v}_i$ and the $j$-th negative occurrence of $v$ by $n^v_j$.
    29	    Note that the obtained formula~$\phiprimer$ contains exclusively negative literals.
    30	
    31	
    32	    For instance, consider the following monotone formula:
    33	    \begin{equation}\label{eq:5}
    34	        \phi = (x \lor y \lor z) \land (\neg x \lor \neg y \lor \neg w) \land (x \lor y \lor w)
    35	    \end{equation}
    36	%
    37	    We get:
    38	    \begin{equation}\label{eq:6}
    39	        \phiprimer = (\neg p^{x}_1 \lor \neg p^{y}_1 \lor \neg p^{z}_1) \land (\neg n^{x}_1 \lor \neg n^{y}_1 \lor \neg n^{w}_1) \land (\neg p^{x}_2 \lor \neg p^{y}_2 \lor \neg p^{w}_1)
    40	    \end{equation}  
    41	%
    42	   We further add clauses to $\phiprimer$ to link the fresh variables to $V$.
    43	    For each variable $v \in V$, let us define a 3-CNF linear formula $\phi'_v$ given below.
    44	    Here, all clauses have two literals and are shown in the form of an implication
    45	    (recall that $x \rightarrow y$ means $\lnot x \lor y$).
    46	    \begin{equation}\label{eq:phi_v}
    47	        \begin{split}
    48	            \phi'_v :=  & (\neg a_1^v \rightarrow v) \land (\neg b_1^v \rightarrow \neg v )\\ 
    49	                    & \land~(\bigwedge^{n}_{i=1} (\lnot p_i^v \rightarrow \neg a_i^v) \land \bigwedge^{n-1}_{i=1} (\neg a_{i+1}^v \rightarrow \neg a_i^v ))\\
    50	                    & \land~(\bigwedge^{m}_{j=1} (\lnot n_j^v \rightarrow \neg b_j^v) \land \bigwedge^{m-1}_{j=1} (\neg b_{j+1}^v \rightarrow \neg b_j^v ))
    51	        \end{split}
    52	    \end{equation}
    53	%
    54	\newcommand{\limply}{\rightarrow}
    55	%
    56	We have, for all $1\leq i \leq n$,
    57	%
    58	\begin{equation}
    59	    \phi'_v \models (\lnot p_i^v \limply v)	\label{eq:implies_pos}
    60	\end{equation}
    61	In fact,  $\phi'_v \models (\lnot p_i^v \rightarrow \lnot a_i^v) \land (\lnot a_i^v \limply \lnot a_{i-1}^v) \land \dots (\lnot a_2^v \limply \lnot a_{1}^v) \land (\lnot a_{1}^v \limply v)$. 
    62	Similarly, 
    63	\begin{equation}
    64	    \phi'_v \models (\lnot n_i^v \limply \lnot v) \label{eq:implies_negat}
    65	\end{equation}
    66	%
    67	We now define $\phi'$ as follows.
    68	%
    69	\begin{equation}\label{eq:7}
    70	    \phi' := \phiprimer \land \bigwedge_{v \in V} \phi'_v
    71	\end{equation}
    72	
    73	We first show that $\phi'$ is a linear literal-planar formula,
    74	and that it is polynomial-time constructible,
    75	and then establish the equisatisfiability of $\phi$ and $\phi'$,
    76	which concludes the proof of the reduction.
    77	
    78	\begin{claim}
    79	    The formula $\phi'$ is linear.
    80	\end{claim}
    81	
    82	\begin{proof}
    83	    Consider the clauses of Equation~\ref{eq:phi_v}. 
    84	    There is an intersection between the clauses $(p_i^v \lor \neg a_i^v)$ and $(\neg a_i^v \lor a_{i+1}^v)$ with a unique literal $\neg a_i^v$.
    85	    There is also an intersection between the clauses $(n_j^v \lor \neg b_j^v)$ and $(\neg b_j^v \lor b_{j+1}^v)$ that contains one literal~$\neg b_j^v$.
    86	    There are no other intersecting clauses; so all intersections do contain at most one literal,
    87	    and each $\phi'_v$ is linear.
    88	    
    89	    In $\phiprimer$, all the occurrences of $v$ and $\neg v$ in $\phi$ are replaced with a unique literal, respectively $\neg p^v_i$ and $\neg n^v_j$, thus there is no intersection between clauses.
    90	    $\phi'_v$ contains only non-negated literals of variables $p_{i}^v$ and $n_{j}^v$ while $\phiprimer$ contains only negated literals of variables $p_{i}^v$ and $n_{j}^v$, thus, there is no intersection between $\bigwedge_{v \in V} \phi'_v$ and $\phiprimer$.
    91	    We conclude that $\phi'$ is a linear 3-CNF formula.
    92	\end{proof}
    93	
    94	
    95	\begin{claim}
    96	    %
    97	    $\litClauGraphSign{\phi',\pi'}$ is valid for some $\pi'$.
    98	\end{claim}
    99	%
   100	\begin{proof}
   101	    %
   102	    We know that $\phi$ is a monotone planar formula w.r.t. $\pi$.
   103	    Thus the \inciGraph $\inciGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ is planar with the variable cycle $\pi$ that separates positive and negative clauses in the considered embedding.
   104	    %
   105	    %
   106	    We are going to construct the \litClausGraph $\litClauGraphSign{\phi', \pi'}$ for some $\pi'$, and prove that it is valid,
   107	    that is, give a planar embedding in which paired edges are inside the literal cycle, and all clauses are outside of it.
   108	    We proceed as follows.
   109	    We modify $\inciGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ by removing each variable node $v$
   110	    and replacing it with $\litClauGraphSign{\phi'_v}$.
   111	    Note that $\litClauGraphSign{\phi'_v}$ contains nodes~$v$ and $\lnot v$.
   112	    The new node $v$ is linked to all positive clauses, while $\lnot v$ is linked to all negative clauses.
   113	    Last, we add all paired edges.
   114	
   115	    The graph $\litClauGraphSign{\phi'_v}$ is made of two disjoint parts,
   116	    the first one containing all nodes connected to $v$, and the second one those connected to $\lnot v$.
   117	    Figure~\ref{fig:lit_cycle_reduc} illustrates the replacement of node $v$ by $\litClauGraphSign{\phi'_v}$.
   118	    A planar embedding for $\litClauGraphSign{\phi'_v}$ is given in
   119	    Figure~\ref{fig:gadget_lit_claus_graph_phi_v}
   120	    where the literals appear along two distinct lines.
   121	    %
   122	    %
   123	    %
   124	
   125	    The literal cycle $\pi'$ is built as follows.
   126	    Let $v_{\pi_1},\ldots,v_{\pi_n}$ denote the variables of~$\phi$ in the order they appear in $\inciGraphSign{\phi, \pi}$.
   127	    $\pi'$ starts with the literals that appear in the upper part of Fig.~\ref{fig:gadget_lit_claus_graph_phi_v} from left to right, first for the variable
   128	    $v_{\pi_1}$, then for $v_{\pi_2}$ etc. until $v_{\pi_n}$.
   129	    Then we add literals from the lower part of Fig.~\ref{fig:gadget_lit_claus_graph_phi_v} from right to left for~$v_{\pi_n}$,
   130	    then $v_{\pi_{n-1}}$, and downto $v_{\pi_1}$.
   131	
   132	    In the presented embedding, the cycle $\pi'$ is such that all paired edges are inside, as seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:gadget_lit_claus_graph_phi_v},
   133	    and all clauses are outside of it. Therefore, 
   134	    $\litClauGraphSign{\phi',\pi'}$ is indeed valid.
   135	    %
   136	    %
   137	    %
   138	    %
   139	
   140	    %
   141	    %
   142	    %
   143	\end{proof}
   144	
   145	
   146	\import{figures_tikz/}{gadget_paired_planar_lsat.tex}
   147	
   148	It follows that $\phi'$ is literal-planar.
   149	Moreover $\phi'$ and $\pi'$ are constructible in polynomial time: we have in total $(2n + 2m)\times|V|+ |C|$ clauses.
   150	
   151	
   152	\begin{claim}
   153	    The formula $\phi$ is satisfiable if and only if $\phi'$ is satisfiable.    
   154	\end{claim}
   155	\begin{proof}
   156		\fbox{$\Rightarrow$}
   157	    Assume $\phi$ is satisfiable, i.e., if there exists a truth assignment $\assignment : V \rightarrow \{0, 1\}$ of $V$ such that each clause $c \in C$ of $\phi$ is satisfied. 
   158	    We show that $\phi'$ is satisfiable. We define a new assignment $\assignment'$ as follows:
   159	    \begin{equation}
   160	    	\begin{split}
   161	            \assignment'(v) = \assignment(v) \\
   162	    		\assignment'(n_j^{v}) =  \assignment'(b_j^{v}) = \assignment(v) \\
   163	    		\assignment'(p_i^{v}) = \assignment'(a_i^{v}) = 1 - \assignment(v)
   164	    	\end{split}
   165	    \label{eq:assignment_paired_lit}
   166	    \end{equation}
   167	    Let us show that $\assignment' \models \phi'$. 
   168	    First, $\assignment' \models \phiprimer$ since $\phiprimer$ is obtained from $\phi$ by replacing each $v$ by some $\lnot p_i^{v}$,
   169	    and $\lnot v$ by some $n_i^{v}$.
   170	    Second, $\assignment' \models \phi'_v$.
   171	    In fact, $\phi'_v$ can be seen as the conjunction of two sequence of implications, namely,
   172	    \[
   173	        \phi'_v \models (\lnot p_i^v \rightarrow \lnot a_i^v) \land (\lnot a_i^v \limply \lnot a_{i-1}^v) \land \dots (\lnot a_2^v \limply \lnot a_{1}^v) \land   (\lnot a_{1}^v \limply v),
   174	    \] 
   175	    and
   176	    \[
   177	        \phi'_v \models (\lnot n_i^v \rightarrow \lnot b_i^v) \land (\lnot b_i^v \limply \lnot b_{i-1}^v) \land \dots (\lnot b_2^v \limply \lnot b_{1}^v) \land   (\lnot b_{1}^v \limply v).
   178	    \] 
   179	    The first set of implications holds because either $\assignment'(v) = 1$ and all variables in this chain are true, 
   180	    or $\assignment'(v) = 0$ and they are all false; and similarly for the second sequence of implications.
   181	
   182	    \fbox{$\Leftarrow$}
   183	    Conversely, consider a truth assignment $\assignment'$ of $V'$ that satisfies $\phi'$. We define $\assignment$ as the restriction of $\assignment'$ to $V$.
   184	    By \eqref{eq:implies_pos} and \eqref{eq:implies_negat}, if $\assignment'(\lnot p_i^v) = 1$ for some $i$, then 
   185	    $\assignment'(\lnot n_j^v) = 0$ for all $j$. In this case, we have $\assignment'(v) = 1$ by \eqref{eq:implies_pos}.    
   186	    Conversely, if $\assignment'(\lnot n_j^v) = 1$ for some $j$,
   187	    then $\assignment'(\lnot p_i^v) = 0$ for all $i$,
   188	    and $\assignment'(v) = 0$.
   189	
   190	    Then because $\assignment' \models \phiprimer$, we have $\assignment \models \phi$ since satisfiability is preserved by replacing each 
   191	    $\lnot p_i^v$ by $v$, and each $\lnot p_j^v$ by $\lnot v$.
   192	\end{proof}
   193	
   194	 We just established that \PLLPTSAT is NP-hard. Because \PLLPTSAT is a fragment of SAT, it is \npc.
   195	%
   196	
   197	
   198	
   199	\subsection{NP-completeness of \LPTSAT}
   200	
   201	\newcommand{\MPLtwoOne}{Monotone Linear Planar\xspace}
   202	
   203	In this section, we prove the NP-hardness for the class of \emph{monotone linear planar 3-CNF} formulas,
   204	a subclass of linear planar 3-CNF formulas.
   205	
   206	\begin{definition}
   207	    \label{def:cmpl}
   208	    A \emph{monotone linear planar 3-CNF} is a linear and monotone planar formula.
   209	    %
   210	    %
   211	    %
   212	    %
   213	\end{definition}
   214	
   215	We now prove the NP-hardness of \MPLtwoOne \tsat by a reduction from \PLLPTSAT. Again \MPLtwoOne \tsat takes both $\phi$ and $\pi$ in the input.
   216	
   217	\newcommand\friendassignment[1]{{#1}^\pm}
   218	
   219	\begin{theorem}
   220		\label{th:constrainedmonotoneplanarlinearthreesatnpcomplete}
   221		\MPLtwoOne \tsat is \npc.
   222	    NP-hardness holds even when each variable appears negatively in at most one clause.
   223	\end{theorem}
   224	
   225	%
   226	
   227	%
   228	Let $\phi$ a \PLLPTformula with $V$ the set of variables, and $C$ the set of clauses. 
   229	For each variable $v$ in $V$ we introduce a fresh variable~$v'$.
   230	%
   231	Define $\psi'$ as follows.
   232	\begin{equation}
   233	    \label{eq:reduction_planar_lsat}
   234	    \psi' := \bigwedge_{v \in V} (\neg v \lor \neg v')
   235	\end{equation}
   236	
   237	
   238	%
   239	The 3-CNF formula $\phi'$ is defined as the conjunction of $\psi'$
   240	with the formula obtained from $\phi$ 
   241	by replacing each $\neg v$ by $v'$, for all $v \in V$.
   242	%
   243	%
   244	
   245	For instance, consider the following \PLLPTformula (Fig.~\ref{fig:planar_paired_lit_lsat_example}):
   246	
   247	\begin{equation}
   248	    \phi := (a \lor \neg b \lor d) \land (b \lor \neg c \lor d) \land (\neg d \lor c) \land (c \lor \neg a)
   249	\end{equation}
   250	
   251	%
   252	We have
   253	
   254	\begin{equation}
   255	    \phi' := (a \lor b' \lor d) \land (b \lor c' \lor d) \land (d' \lor c) \land (c \lor a') \land \psi'
   256	\end{equation}
   257	
   258	
   259	\import{figures_tikz/}{reduction_paired_to_planar_lsat.tex}
   260	
   261	
   262	We can verify that $\phi'$ is a linear 3-CNF: $\phi$ is a linear 3-CNF and replacing each negated literal by new variables preserve linearity. 
   263	Furthermore, clauses of $\psi'$ do not intersect each other,
   264	and the clauses of $\phi'$ and $\psi'$ also do not intersect since the former contains only positive literals, and the latter only negative ones.
   265	
   266	Recall that $\litClauGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ is planar and valid, by hypothesis.
   267	Consider the variable cycle $\pi'$ obtained from $\pi$ by replacing each $\lnot v$ by $v'$.
   268	We argue that $\inciGraphSign{\phi',\pi'}$ is planar. 
   269	In fact, $\inciGraphSign{\phi',\pi'}$ can be obtained from $\litClauGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ 
   270	by renaming each node $\lnot v$  into $v'$, and adding the new clauses $\lnot v \lor \lnot v'$.
   271	Because $\litClauGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ is valid, all clauses are outside of the literal cycle.
   272	It follows that in $\inciGraphSign{\phi',\pi'}$, all positive clauses of $\pi'$ are outside the variable cycle $\pi$,
   273	while the negative clauses of $\psi$ are placed inside the cycle.
   274	Moreover, each negative clause $\lnot v \lor \lnot v'$ can be seen as replacing a paired edge between $v$ and $\lnot v$.
   275	Therefore $\litClauGraphSign{\phi,\pi}$ is planar and valid.
   276	
   277	An example of this transformation is provided in %
   278	Fig.~\ref{fig:reduction_paired_lit_plsat_to_planar_lsat}.
   279	
   280	%
   281	%
   282	
   283	%
   284	
   285	Note that our construction is a monotone linear planar 3-CNF:
   286	each clause contains only negated or only positive literals,
   287	and the positive clauses are separated from the negative clauses by the variable cycle.
   288	
   289	
   290	\begin{claim}
   291	   Formula $\phi$ is satisfiable if and only if $\phi'$ is satisfiable.
   292	    \label{lem:linear_literal_planar_sat_iff_linear_lanar_sat}
   293	\end{claim}
   294	
   295	\begin{proof}
   296	    \fbox{$\Rightarrow$}
   297	    Assume $\phi$ is satisfiable, i.e. there exists a truth assignment $\nu$ for $V$ such that each clause $c$ of $\phi$ is satisfied. 
   298	    We define $\friendassignment\nu$ as the 
   299	    \emph{friend assignment} obtained from $\nu$ as follows. 
   300	    For each $v \in V$,
   301	
   302	    \begin{equation}
   303	        \begin{split}
   304	            \friendassignment\nu(v) := \nu(v) \\
   305	            \friendassignment\nu(v') := 1 - \nu(v)
   306	        \end{split}
   307	    \label{eq:assignment_paired_lit}
   308	    \end{equation}
   309	
   310	    We have $\friendassignment\nu \models (\neg v \lor \neg v')$ for all variable $v$. Furthermore, the transformed clauses are also made true by $\friendassignment\nu$. So $\friendassignment\nu \models \phi'$, and $\phi'$ is satisfiable.
   311	
   312	    \fbox{$\Leftarrow$}
   313	    Conversely, let $\nu'$ be a truth assignment for $V'$ that satisfies $\phi'$. 
   314	    Let $\nu$ denote the restriction of $\nu'$ to $V$.
   315	    
   316	    All transformed clauses of $\phi'$ are satisfied by $\nu'$.
   317	    Let $c$ be a clause of $\phi$ and $c'$ the corresponding clause of $\phi'$ obtained from $c$.
   318	    Let $\ell \in c'$ such that $\nu' \models \ell$.
   319	    If $\ell$ is in $V$, then it also appears in $c$, and $\nu(\ell) = \nu'(\ell)$, thus $\nu \models c$.
   320	    Otherwise, $\ell = v'$ for some variable $v \in V$, and $c$ contains the literal $\lnot v$.
   321	    But the clause $\lnot v \lor \lnot v'$ of $\phi'$ implies that in this case, $\nu'(v)=0$, that is, $\nu(v) = 0$.
   322	    It follows that $\nu \models c$.
   323	    
   324	    Hence, $\phi$ is satisfiable.
   325	\end{proof}
   326	
   327	\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}[theorem]
   328	
   329	
   330	In conclusion, we proved that \MPLtwoOne is NP-complete. 
   331	As a corollary, we get our main result stated in the following theorem.
   332	
   333	\begin{theorem}
   334		\LPTSAT is \npc.
   335		\label{th:planar_linear_tsat_npc}
   336	\end{theorem}

codex
The paper’s key restriction is stricter and more specific than ordinary “linear hypergraph SAT”: clauses may share the same signed literal with at most one other clause, while opposite literals are distinct for that condition. Its strongest native SAT theorem is Theorem 3.2: monotone linear planar 3-SAT remains NP-complete even when every variable has at most one negative occurrence. I’m now checking whether that theorem yields a scalable planted distribution or only worst-case hardness, and whether the planning reduction offers a better fixed-size witness.
