     4	In this section, we introduce the \emph{planar cycle augmentation} problem.
     5	This consists, given a planar graph $G$ and a subset $V'$ of vertices, in computing a $V'$-cycle\footnote{i.e. a cycle among vertices in $V'$}~$\genericcycle$ that can be added to $G$ while preserving planarity. 
     6	
     7	\begin{definition}[Planar Cycle Augmentation]
     8	    Planar cycle augmentation is the following decision problem:
     9	    \begin{itemize}
    10	        \item Instance: an undirected planar graph $G = (V,E)$, a subset $V' \subseteq V$.
    11	        \item Question: is there a $V'$-cycle $\genericcycle$ such that the multi-graph $G' = (V, E \uplus \genericcycle)$ is planar?
    12	    \end{itemize}
    13	    \label{def:planar_cycle_augment_decision}
    14	\end{definition}
    15	
    16	Planar cycle augmentation is the cornerstone of this article. 
    17	In \Cref{sec:PLLPTSATreconfiguration}, a $V'$-cycle corresponds to a literal cycle in the \PLLPTSAT Reconfiguration problem.
    18	The existence of a literal cycle will be crucial for proving the PSPACE-completeness of 2D CMAPF (\Cref{th:2D CMAPFPSPACEcomplete}). 
    19	In fact, the exact complexity of 2D CMAPF was left open in \cite{cmapfIsse}
    20	because existing planar 3-SAT reconfiguration problems in the literature~\cite{monotonNAEtSATRecon} are not sufficiently powerful as they do not mention any notion of variable or literal cycle.
    21	The developments of this section allows us to define a reconfiguration problem powerful enough to fill this gap.
    22	
    23	\Cref{fig:cycle_augment_example} shows an example of an instance of planar cycle augmentation. 
    24	The graph augmented with a cycle $\genericcycle$ can be seen on the right in the figure.
