    35	\subsection{NP-completeness of Cycle augmentation}
    36	
    37	\begin{theorem}
    38	    \emph{Planar cycle augmentation} is \npc.
    39	
    40	    \label{th:general_planar_cycle_construction_npc}
    41	\end{theorem}
    42	
    43	\begin{proof}
    44	    \emph{Planar cycle augmentation} is in NP by guessing a $V'$-cycle and checking the planarity of $G'$ in polynomial time \cite{hopcroft1974efficient}.    
    45	    We prove the NP-hardness of \cycleaugment by reducing from the following problem: decide if an arbitrary maximal\footnote{maximal means that adding another edge to the graph would break planarity} planar graph is \emph{Hamiltonian} \cite{hamiltonianMaximalPlanarDecPbNPC}. 
    46	    Consider a maximal planar graph $G = (V,E)$. The planar cycle augmentation instance is $(G, V)$.
    47	    As $G$ is a maximal planar graph, for all cycle $\genericcycle$, $G' = (V, E \uplus \genericcycle)$ is planar iff $\genericcycle \subseteq E$.
    48	    Hence, finding a $V'$-cycle $\genericcycle$ is equivalent to finding an \emph{Hamiltonian cycle} within the graph $G$.
    49	    Hence \cycleaugment is NP-hard.
    50	\end{proof}
