   292	\begin{claim}[Soundness]
   293	   \Cref{alg:constructingVprimecycle} returns a $V'$-cycle $\genericcycle$ such that $(V, E \uplus \genericcycle)$ is planar. Moreover, $\acycle$ separates the red and blue edges.
   294	\end{claim}
   295	
   296	\begin{proof}
   297	    At the end, $\gvf(i)$ has two faces. By \Cref{prop:planar_eulerian_g_two_faces_is_cycle}, $\gvf(i)$ is a cycle.
   298	    The final step consists in edge smoothing (\Cref{def:edge_smoothing}) to remove vertices not in $V'$ from $\gvf(i)$: we get a $V'$-cycle $\genericcycle$. 
   299	    
   300	    By \Cref{fact:vertexspliting}, $\Pi_{G,\gvf(i)}= \Pi_{G,\gvf}$. The cycle $K(i)$ respects the partition $\Pi_{G,\gvf}$ meaning the red $G$-edges w.r.t. $K_I$ are inside while the blue ones are outside, up to color renaming. So the $V'$-cycle $\pi$ also respects this partition.
   301	    As $G \cup \gvf(i)$ is planar so is $G \cup \genericcycle$.
   302	\end{proof}
