   310	\subsection{A Feasible Class of Instances}
   311	
   312	\newcommand{\specialInst}{dually connected matching\xspace}
   313	\newcommand{\edgedeletion}{{-}}
   314	
   315	
   316	In this section we introduce a class of instances for which a $V'$-cycle  $\acycle$ exists and can be computed in polynomial time.
   317	%
   318	%
   319	These instances are those which have a \emph{\specialInst}.
   320	
   321	\begin{definition}[Dually Connected Matching]
   322	    Let $I = (G = (V,E),V')$ be a cycle augmentation instance.
   323	    A subset $E'\subseteq E \cap V' \times V'$ of edges is a \emph{dually connected matching} for $I$ if:
   324	    \begin{enumerate}
   325	    	\item $E'$ is a $V'$-perfect matching: for all $v \in V'$ there is an edge $e \in E'$ incident to $v$, and for all $e, e' \in E'$, if $e \neq e'$ then $e \cap e' = \emptyset$.
   326	        %
   327	        \item $E'$ is dually connected: the graph $G_{E'}^*$ obtained from $G^*$ by only keeping the dual edges of edges in
   328	$E'$ is connected\footnote{i.e. 
   329	          $G^* \edgedeletion \bijectionedgetodualG(E \setminus E')$
   330	           is connected where
   331	           $\bijectionedgetodualG(E \setminus E') := \set{\bijectionedgetodualG(e) \mid e \in (E \setminus E')}$.
   332	           See \Cref{def:edge_deletion} for the definition of the edge deletion operation $\edgedeletion$.}.
   333	        
   334	    \end{enumerate}
   335	    \label{def:SepcialInst}
   336	\end{definition}
   337	
   338	
   339	In \Cref{def:SepcialInst}, point 1 says that $E'$ is a $V'$-perfect matching meaning that $E'$ touch all vertices in $V'$, and edges in $E'$ do not share common vertices. Point 2 says that when the dual graph $G^*$ remains connected even if we only keep the dual edges of edges in $E'$ (recall that a dual edge of $e$ is the edge linking the two faces touching $e$). \Cref{fig:dualg_edge_coll} gives an example of a dually connected matching.
