+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,230 @@
+# Monotone linear planar SAT with a hidden residue invariant
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | logic |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate | Boolean integer tuple, serialized as one bitstring |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: pair tags split into multiplicative square cosets |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 3, Theorem 3.2 (the displayed `psi'` and friend-assignment formulas) |
+
+This generator is based on Desbois, Sankur, and Schwarzentruber,
+[*Linear Planar 3-SAT and Its Applications in Planning*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.14713).
+A solver receives a monotone linear planar 3-CNF formula, its variable cycle,
+and tagged complementary variable pairs. It must return a complete Boolean
+assignment. `verify` evaluates every literal and clause exactly, so checking a
+candidate is linear in the displayed formula.
+
+The construction is native to the paper's logic objects and reaches the target
+through its central licensed transformation. A noncrossing linear literal-planar source
+formula is built around a legal orientation, then Theorem 3.2 replaces every
+negative source literal `not-v` by a positive variable `v'` and adds
+`(not-v or not-v')`. The proof's friend assignment is carried through. The
+generator never solves the emitted instance to obtain its answer.
+
+## Trust status
+
+All local correctness, corruption, density, adversary, scaling, canonical-key,
+and size/effort gates pass. The mandatory external oracle claim is **not yet
+available**: all bare, structural-hint, and placebo calls returned OpenRouter
+HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. The harness correctly counted
+these as errors rather than failures. Consequently this directory is a fully
+reproducible candidate generator, but it is not valid evidence that the oracle
+pool was defeated; rerun `harden.py` with a refreshed OpenRouter key before
+submission.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 2 fixes the exact definitions: clauses have at most three literals;
+linearity concerns intersections as sets of **signed** literals; and planarity
+uses the variable-clause graph augmented by a variable cycle. Section 3,
+Theorem 3.2 proves NP-completeness even for monotone linear planar formulas
+with at most one negative occurrence per variable. That is the regime emitted
+here, but worst-case hardness is not claimed for this planted distribution.
+
+An efficient distribution-specific algorithm exists and is disclosed. Square
+the integers `1..63` modulo `127`; the first member of a displayed pair is true
+exactly when its tag is in that residue set, and its mate has the opposite bit.
+This costs 63 modular squarings plus 126 membership decisions, or 189 exact
+operations. The domain-standard unit-propagating DPLL reference also succeeds
+on 8/8 shipping instances. It has `O(2^N M)` worst-case complexity and measured
+81,018 literal checks, 89.6 search nodes, and 0.014 seconds on average here.
+That successful algorithm is reported separately, as Track B requires.
+
+The compact route is short enough to execute without a SAT engine, while
+mechanically propagating hundreds of shuffled clauses is not. This is a
+no-tool compression claim, not a complexity-theoretic claim. The paper itself
+does not give a polynomial special-case solver for linear planar SAT; its
+polynomial result in Section 4, Theorem 4.2 concerns constructing cycle
+augmentations, not solving these formulas.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` instance below (`n=6`, seed 0) is hand-solvable. Its planted answer
+is `011100100011`; direct verification accepts it and rejects a dropped final
+bit with a precise length error.
+
+```python
+>>> inst = make_instance(n=6, or_percent=35, seed=0)
+>>> verify(inst, "011100100011")
+(True, 'ok')
+>>> verify(inst, "01110010001")
+(False, 'answer is too short: expected 12 bits')
+```
+
+Full rendered demo instance:
+
+```text
+MONOTONE LINEAR PLANAR SAT
+
+There are 12 Boolean variables, numbered 1 through
+12.  A positive literal +i has the value of variable i;
+a negative literal -i has the opposite value.  A clause is true when at least
+one of its listed literals is true, and the formula is true when every clause
+is true.
+
+For this problem, 3-CNF means clauses of size at most three, as in the source
+paper; this instance uses clauses of sizes two and three.  Every clause is
+monotone (both signs agree).  The formula is linear in the paper's exact sense:
+each clause, regarded as a set of signed literals, intersects at most one other
+clause and that intersection has size at most one.
+
+The listed VARIABLE CYCLE is a permutation of all variables.  It is part of a
+planar embedding of the variable-clause graph: that bipartite graph has one
+node per variable and clause, incidence edges join clauses to their variables,
+and the cycle edges join consecutive listed variables including last to first.
+The embedding is specified topologically as follows.  Draw the variable cycle
+as a circle.  Draw every negative clause inside as the subdivision of the chord
+between its tagged pair; those chords are noncrossing.  Draw each connected
+component of positive clauses outside, in the unique three-consecutive-variable
+arc containing all of its variables; these arcs are disjoint.  Thus this is a
+monotone linear planar instance in the paper's native regime.
+
+Each PAIR TAG row `(a,b):r` records the two variables in one negative pair
+clause and a distinct nonzero tag r modulo p=7.  Tags are exact
+instance data.  Variable IDs, not tags, index the answer.
+
+PAIR TAGS (first variable, second variable):tag
+  (2,8):1  (6,12):5  (3,9):4  (5,11):3  (1,7):6  (4,10):2
+
+VARIABLE CYCLE (continued lines form one cyclic order)
+  2 6 9 3 1 5 11 7 10 4 12 8
+
+CLAUSES
+C1: -4 -10
+C2: -6 -12
+C3: +6 +2
+C4: +10 +7 +11
+C5: -2 -8
+C6: -11 -5
+C7: +3 +1
+C8: +5 +3
+C9: +12 +4
+C10: +8 +12
+C11: -9 -3
+C12: +9 +2
+C13: -1 -7
+
+Return one bit for every variable in increasing ID order: bit i is 1 exactly
+when variable i is TRUE.  The answer must therefore be exactly
+12 characters long and contain only 0 and 1.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as the raw bitstring.
+Example for four variables: <answer>0101</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+Counts below use seed 0; clause counts vary slightly with the sampled mix of
+OR and AND components. After `hard`, `escalate()` first tightens the formula at
+fixed answer length by lowering `or_percent` through 20, 10, and 0. Only after
+that axis is exhausted does it return `"cap_bound"`, because the next
+admissible size would exceed 256 answer bits.
+
+| preset | source pairs `n` | answer variables | clauses at seed 0 | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 12 | 13 | paper example / hand scale |
+| easy | 30 | 60 | 64 | oracle unavailable |
+| medium | 60 | 120 | 129 | not reached |
+| hard | 126 | 252 | 266 | shipping candidate |
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses; 12/12 exact class checks; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | empty, short, long, nonbinary, and one-pair swap rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | fenced, prose-surrounded answer round-trips exactly |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses at shipping |
+| G5 | pass | shipping sampled fraction 0/200,000; demo exact count 21/729; reference about 81,107 operations |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks, each 0/8; DPLL reference 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | doubled request builds 540 variables and verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 254 serialized characters, about 64 tokens, 252 bits, 189 intended operations |
+
+The five failing G6 attacks are positive-occurrence outlier selection,
+one-pass greedy clause repair, 256 structure-aware random restarts, the
+all-first-pair ansatz, and tag parity. The successful DPLL reference is not in
+`attacks`, because Track B requires it under `reference_algorithm`.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+No row below is a solve or a failure; all are service errors and therefore give
+no hardness evidence.
+
+| preset | seed | model | result | why |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1360808558 | google/gemini-3.8-flash | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 584323074 | google/gemini-3.8-flash | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 519137594 | google/gemini-3.8-flash | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1021683200 | google/gemini-3.8-flash | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+## G9 diagnostic arms
+
+| arm | solved / valid attempts | diagnostic |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
+
+`hinted − placebo` is unavailable, not zero: neither arm obtained a valid
+attempt. The intended structural hint only names the quadratic-residue
+invariant. It does not chain a procedure. The answer and effort measurements
+are independent of the unavailable oracle service: 254 characters, 252 atomic
+bits, and 189 exact operations.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2506_14713 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=42, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+prompt = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>" + inst["answer"] + "</answer>")
+assert verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After obtaining a valid script-owned oracle verdict, emit from the repository
+root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2506.14713
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The tags intentionally make this distribution easy for software and for a
+solver that notices the residue invariant; that is the Track B claim. The
+paper's NP-completeness theorem is worst-case and does not establish hardness
+of these generated instances. The 0/200,000 estimate is under the explicit
+prior that independently samples `00`, `01`, or `10` for every negative pair;
+it says nothing about DPLL, CDCL, treewidth dynamic programming, or the hidden
+tag prior. An industrial CDCL solver and a specialized outerplanar dynamic
+program were not tested. The canonical key is a strong colored
+Weisfeiler–Lehman invariant, not a complete isomorphism canonizer, so rare
+nonisomorphic collisions are possible. Most importantly, oracle hardness
+remains unverified until the OpenRouter account limit is repaired.
