src/main.tex:202:The boolean satisfiability problem (SAT) is a major tool to classify decision problems with respect to their feasibility.
src/main.tex:205:This is the case, for example, of hard problems on \emph{planar} graphs.
src/main.tex:206:While some NP-hard problems such as node cover, and directed Hamiltonian path remain NP-hard when the input graph is assumed to be planar~\cite{lichtenstein1982planar},
src/main.tex:207:existing polynomial reductions from SAT for general graphs do not produce planar graphs, and proving hardness for planar graphs
src/main.tex:208:thus requires designing reductions adapted for planar graphs. 
src/main.tex:209:This has motivated defining subclasses of the SAT problem. Accordingly, \cite{lichtenstein1982planar} first defines the  NP-complete \emph{planar SAT} problem 
src/main.tex:210:which is the SAT problem restricted to instances that induce a planar \emph{incidence} graph\footnote{This is a bipartite graph connecting clauses to variables they contain,
src/main.tex:212:Once the NP-completeness of planar SAT is established, then reductions to planar graph problems are done easily.
src/main.tex:213:Thus planar SAT has become a natural tool for proving the hardness of planar graph problems \cite{dyer1986planar,mahajan2012planar,shi2005rectilinear} but also planning problems which are naturally cast in planar domains~\cite{yu2015intractability,adler2020tatamibari,allen2018sto}.
src/main.tex:217:graph problems \cite{arkin2018selecting,simon2021algorithmic} and also applied to planning problems;
src/main.tex:219:Other applications in planning include the complexity of multi-agent path execution with uncertainty~\cite{liu2024multi}. As for the planar case, resulting reductions from linear SAT are much more natural than encoding the general SAT problem in the graph or planning problems at hand.
src/main.tex:221:Another type of problems related to planning is the \emph{SAT reconfiguration} problem.
src/main.tex:226:The \emph{3-SAT reconfiguration} problem consists in solving this for 3-CNF formulas, and is PSPACE-complete~\cite{monotonNAEtSATRecon}.
src/main.tex:227:Planning problems in which the arena is succinctly described (by a Boolean formula) can naturally be reduced to SAT reconfiguration problems. It was shown that the planar 3-SAT reconfiguration problem is PSPACE-complete \cite{reconfigComplexity}.
src/main.tex:228:Note that reconfiguration problems are not restricted to SAT; one can lift any decision problem to a reconfiguration problem; \cite{reconfigComplexity} shows that many reconfiguration problems derived from NP-complete problems are PSPACE-complete.
src/main.tex:230:In this paper, we investigate subclasses of the SAT and SAT reconfiguration problems restricted to both planar \emph{and} linear formulas. Our goal is to investigate restricted classes of these problems that are still NP-complete and PSPACE-complete respectively, while providing new tools for proving the hardness of planning problems;
src/main.tex:233:For the SAT problem, we present two variants: \emph{linear planar 3-SAT} and \emph{linear literal-planar 3-SAT}.
src/main.tex:234:Both require formulas to be linear but both make planarity assumptions on different graphs.
src/main.tex:235:Linear planar 3-SAT is the 3-SAT problem restricted to formulas that are both linear and planar.
src/main.tex:236:Therefore, it requires that the \emph{variable-clause graph} augmented with a \emph{variable cycle} is planar~\cite{lichtenstein1982planar}.
src/main.tex:238:In linear literal-planar 3-SAT, 
src/main.tex:239:we require that the \emph{literal-clause graph} augmented with a \emph{literal cycle} is planar.
src/main.tex:245:We prove that both new variants are NP-complete. We obtain this result first for linear literal-planar 3-SAT by reduction 
src/main.tex:246:from monotone planar 3-SAT \cite{de2010optimal}; then we reduce linear literal-planar 3-SAT to 
src/main.tex:247:monotone linear planar 3-SAT which is a restriction of linear planar 3-SAT to monotone formulas.
src/main.tex:248:The NP-hardness of linear planar 3-SAT follows.
src/main.tex:250:We also study the complexity of the reconfiguration problems derived from both variants of 3-SAT
src/main.tex:251:and prove them to be PSPACE-complete following a similar path. We first prove the PSPACE-hardness of
src/main.tex:252:linear literal-planar 3-SAT reconfiguration by reduction from NCL~\cite{gamesNCLthesis}, and then reduce it to monotone linear planar 3-SAT reconfiguration,
src/main.tex:253:which is a subset of the linear planar 3-SAT reconfiguration.
